The youthfulness of the population of The Gambia can be attributed to the decline in
mortality among children. By implication, the high proportion of the population between the
ages 15–24 years in The Gambia would continue to grow even if fertility declines slightly. As
these people are within the reproductive age groups, sooner or later they would start
procreating and the population would continue to grow. The youthfulness of the population
also has socio-economic implications for development ranging from the building of more
schools, investments in health care, job creation and the provision of skills acquisition
centres.
Population pyramids are of different shapes and are determined by three factors: fertility,
mortality and migration. The population pyramids with broad bases narrowing towards the
older ages are typical of countries in the early stage of demographic development with high
fertility and mortality rates and a youthful age structure. The age and sex pyramids of
African countries have this type of shape.
