2.4 Distribution of the population
As the trend in most developing countries of Sub-Saharan Africa, there has been a rapid
increase in the urban population due mainly to the high internal migration from the rural
areas and immigrants from outside The Gambia. Until the mid-1970s rural-urban migration in
The Gambia was of a seasonal nature when rural dwellers migrated to the urban areas in
search of work during the dry season and returned to the rural areas to farm as the rainy
season starts. Consecutive years of drought in the late 1970s and the resultant effects of
reduced agricultural outputs triggered a new migratory trend. Since this period, many rural
migrants decided to stay in the urban areas.
2.4.1 Population distribution by residence and LGA
At the national level, the proportion of the population residing in urban areas has increased
from 50.4 per cent in 2003 to 57.8 per cent in 2013. Among the LGAs which have rural
characteristics Brikama has the highest rate of urbanization. For instance, in 2003, 60.3 per
cent and 39.7 per cent of the population resided in the urban and rural areas respectively;
compared to 73.6 er cent and 26.4 per cent of the population who lived in the urban and rural
areas respectively in 2013. The Kuntaur, Janjanbureh and Mansakonko LGAs are the least
urbanized with 92.7, 85.1 and 81.4 per cent respectively of their population living in the rural
areas (Table 2.4.1 below).
