2.5 Sex ratio
Sex ratio less than 100 indicates that there are relatively more females, whilst a sex ratio
greater than 100 indicates that there are relatively more males than females. In general, sex
ratios below 90 or above 110 are usually the result of migration, abnormally high levels of
mortality caused by wars or disease epidemics, an undercount or over count of one of the
sexes.
2.5.1 Sex ratio by Local Government Area
Table 2.5.1 below shows that the sex ratio for The Gambia in 2013 was 97 males per 100
females. Banjul had the highest sex ratio of 118 males per 100 females. Given that Banjul has
had a negative growth rate in the last three decades due mainly to male out-migration to
Kanifing, this unusually high sex ratio suggests either an under-count of the female
population or an over-count of the male population. The Mansakonko, Kerewan, Kuntaur,
Janjanbureh and Basse LGAs all had sex ratios ranging from 90 males per 100 females.
