CHAPTER 3: ETHINICITY AND NATIONALITY
3.1 Population distribution by ethnicity
The Gambia like most African countries has several ethnic groups. In the 2013 Census, nine
ethnic groups were identified and the rest were classified under “other ethnic groups".
Information on ethnicity was only collected from the Gambian population.
Table 3.1.1 shows the distribution of the population by ethnicity for 1983, 1993, 2003 and
2013. Mandinka’s constituted the largest ethnic group in the 2013 with 34.4 per cent.
However, the proportions of Mandinkas have been declining over the years as the other
ethnic groups (Fula and Wollof) also increase their percentage share of the overall
population. The Second largest ethnic group was Fula/Tukulor/ Lorobo with 24.1 per cent.
This is followed by Wollof and Jola with 14.8 per cent and 10.5 per cent of the population
respectively. The Sarehulehs and Sereres constitute 8.2 per cent and 3.1 per cent of the
population respectively, while the Creole/Aku Marabous accounted for the least ethnic group
with 0.5 per cent.
